# 07 - Column Functions (PySpark Practical)

This practical covers most used column functions:
- col(), lit()
- when() otherwise()
- concat(), concat_ws()
- substring(), trim(), lower(), upper()
- regexp_replace(), regexp_extract()
- split(), array_contains()

---

## 0) Setup

```python
from pyspark.sql import SparkSession
from pyspark.sql.functions import (
    col, lit, when,
    concat, concat_ws,
    substring, trim, lower, upper,
    regexp_replace, regexp_extract,
    split, array_contains
)

spark = SparkSession.builder     .appName("ColumnFunctionsPractical")     .master("local[*]")     .getOrCreate()

data = [
    (1, "  Pramod  ", "IT", "salary:50000", "Python,SQL,PySpark"),
    (2, "Akash", "HR", "salary:60000", "Excel,Recruitment"),
    (3, "rahul", "IT", "salary:45000", "SQL,PowerBI"),
    (4, "Neha", "Finance", "salary:70000", "Finance,Excel"),
]

df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ["id", "name", "dept", "salary_text", "skills"])
df.show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 1) col() and lit()

```python
df.select(col("name"), lit("India").alias("country")).show()
```

---

## 2) when() otherwise() (IF-ELSE)

Create salary_band using extracted salary.

First extract salary number:

```python
df2 = df.withColumn("salary", regexp_extract(col("salary_text"), r"(\d+)", 1).cast("int"))
df2.show(truncate=False)
```

Now salary band:

```python
df3 = df2.withColumn(
    "salary_band",
    when(col("salary") >= 60000, "HIGH")
    .when(col("salary") >= 50000, "MEDIUM")
    .otherwise("LOW")
)

df3.select("name", "salary", "salary_band").show()
```

---

## 3) concat() and concat_ws()

### 3.1 concat()

```python
df3.withColumn("emp_tag", concat(col("dept"), lit("_"), col("name"))).show(truncate=False)
```

---

### 3.2 concat_ws() (best for joining strings)

```python
df3.withColumn("emp_tag", concat_ws("-", col("dept"), col("name"))).show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 4) substring(), trim(), lower(), upper()

```python
df_clean = df.withColumn("name_trim", trim(col("name")))              .withColumn("name_lower", lower(trim(col("name"))))              .withColumn("name_upper", upper(trim(col("name"))))              .withColumn("name_short", substring(trim(col("name")), 1, 3))

df_clean.select("name", "name_trim", "name_lower", "name_upper", "name_short").show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 5) regexp_replace(), regexp_extract()

### 5.1 Remove word "salary:" using regexp_replace

```python
df_regex = df.withColumn("salary_num", regexp_replace(col("salary_text"), "salary:", "").cast("int"))
df_regex.select("salary_text", "salary_num").show(truncate=False)
```

### 5.2 Extract only digits using regexp_extract

```python
df_regex2 = df.withColumn("salary_num", regexp_extract(col("salary_text"), r"(\d+)", 1).cast("int"))
df_regex2.select("salary_text", "salary_num").show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 6) split(), array_contains()

Split skills into array:

```python
df_skills = df.withColumn("skills_arr", split(col("skills"), ","))
df_skills.select("name", "skills_arr").show(truncate=False)
```

Check if skill contains SQL:

```python
df_skills.filter(array_contains(col("skills_arr"), "SQL")).show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 7) Summary

- col() is used to reference columns.
- lit() creates constant column.
- when/otherwise = IF-ELSE.
- concat and concat_ws join strings.
- trim/lower/upper clean text.
- regexp_replace/extract handle patterns.
- split + array_contains is used for arrays.

